"""Scene definitions for the Authentication vs Authorization teaching video (generated from concept.toml)."""

SCENES = [
    dict(key='01-poster', kind='poster', title='Authentication vs Authorization',
         body=None,
         narration="Hello, and welcome. [[slnc 400]] This video explains authentication and authorization, in Java. [[slnc 300]] This video is presented by Jayasekhar Konduru. [[slnc 600]] First, a simple definition. [[slnc 300]] Authentication asks, who are you? [[slnc 300]] It is proved with something only that person should have, like a password. [[slnc 300]] Authorization asks, what may you do? [[slnc 300]] It is decided by rules about roles, and about who owns what. [[slnc 600]] Think of a hotel. [[slnc 300]] At the front desk, the clerk checks your passport, and gives you a key card. [[slnc 300]] That is authentication. [[slnc 300]] The key card opens your room, and the gym. [[slnc 300]] But not the other guests' rooms, and not the staff office. [[slnc 300]] That is authorization. [[slnc 700]] In this video, the domain is an online store, with customers, support staff, and an admin. [[slnc 500]] By the end, you will hear why a login is not enough. [[slnc 300]] The difference between a four oh one and a four oh three. [[slnc 300]] And what the checks cost."),
    dict(key='02-scenario', kind='bullets', title='The Scenario',
         body=['Customers: Ana and Ben', 'Support: Sam', 'Admin: Alex', '', 'Customers manage their own orders.', 'Support views any order, and refunds.', 'Admin may do everything.'],
         narration='Here is the scenario. [[slnc 400]] An online store has three kinds of people. [[slnc 500]] Customers, Ana and Ben, may view and cancel their own orders. [[slnc 500]] Support staff, Sam, may view any order, and issue refunds. [[slnc 500]] And an admin, Alex, may do everything. [[slnc 600]] The shop must tell these people apart, and give each of them only what their role allows.'),
    dict(key='03-two-questions', kind='bullets', title='Two Questions',
         body=['Authentication', '  who are you?', '  401: I do not know who you are', '', 'Authorization', '  what may you do?', '  403: I know who you are, and no'],
         narration='Before the demos, the two questions. [[slnc 500]] Authentication. [[slnc 300]] Who are you? [[slnc 300]] When it fails, the answer is four oh one. [[slnc 300]] I do not know who you are. [[slnc 600]] Authorization. [[slnc 300]] What may you do? [[slnc 300]] When it fails, the answer is four oh three. [[slnc 300]] I know who you are, and the answer is no. [[slnc 600]] The names of the two codes are famously confusing. [[slnc 300]] Four oh one says unauthorized, but means unauthenticated. [[slnc 300]] Both are H T T P status codes, the numbers a web server answers with.'),
    dict(key='04-letters-1', kind='bullets', title='What the Letters Stand For',
         body=['AuthN: authentication: who are you?', 'AuthZ: authorization: what may you do?', 'HTTP: HyperText Transfer Protocol', 'OK: status 200: the request was done', 'PBKDF2: Password-Based Key Derivation Function 2', 'HMAC: Hash-based Message Authentication Code'],
         narration='A few letters come up again and again, so here is what they stand for. [[slnc 400]] Auth N, with an N, is short for authentication: who are you? [[slnc 400]] Auth Z, with a Z, is short for authorization: what may you do? [[slnc 400]] H T T P is HyperText Transfer Protocol, the language of the web. Its status codes are numbers like two hundred, four oh one and four oh three. [[slnc 400]] O K, two hundred, means the request was done. [[slnc 400]] P B K D F two is Password Based Key Derivation Function two: a slow, salted way to hash a password. [[slnc 400]] H M A C is Hash based Message Authentication Code: a hash mixed with a key. P B K D F two uses it in every round.'),
    dict(key='05-letters-2', kind='bullets', title='What the Letters Stand For (continued)',
         body=['SHA-256: Secure Hash Algorithm, 256-bit', 'hex: hexadecimal: each byte as two characters, 00 to ff'],
         narration='S H A two five six is Secure Hash Algorithm, two hundred and fifty six bit: the hash inside H M A C. [[slnc 400]] And hex is short for hexadecimal: every byte is written as two characters, from zero zero to f f.'),
    dict(key='06-core', kind='figure', title='One gate, two questions',
         body='two_questions',
         narration="Let's start with the whole idea, on three real requests from the demo. [[slnc 400]] Every request meets the gate, which asks two questions, in order. [[slnc 600]] Question one, authentication: who are you? [[slnc 300]] The first request has no token. [[slnc 300]] Nobody is known, so it stops here, with four oh one. [[slnc 600]] The second request carries Ben's token, tok one. [[slnc 300]] Ben is known, so it goes on to question two, authorization: may Ben do this? [[slnc 300]] It asks to view order O one, which is Ana's. [[slnc 300]] No. [[slnc 300]] Four oh three. [[slnc 600]] The third request is Ben viewing his own order, O two. [[slnc 300]] Known, and allowed. [[slnc 300]] Two hundred, okay. [[slnc 600]] Keep these two questions in mind. [[slnc 300]] The demos build the gate one question at a time."),
    dict(key='07-act-1-figure', kind='figure', title='No checks: every request gets through',
         body='no_checks',
         narration="First demo: the shop with no checks. [[slnc 400]] On the left, in green, the requests. [[slnc 300]] On the right, in red, the responses. [[slnc 600]] Two requests arrive. [[slnc 300]] Neither has a login, or a token. [[slnc 300]] The first asks to refund order O one, Ana's laptop, twelve hundred dollars. [[slnc 300]] The second asks to view the same order. [[slnc 600]] In the middle is the gate. [[slnc 300]] In this version, it asks no questions at all. [[slnc 600]] So both responses are two hundred, okay. [[slnc 300]] Anyone on the internet can refund Ana's laptop, and read her order. [[slnc 300]] The shop has no idea that people exist."),
    dict(key='08-act-1', kind='console', title='Act One — The first version, with no checks',
         body="ONE. The first version, with no checks.\n  THE REQUESTS (no login, no token):\n    1  refund O-1  (ana's laptop, 1200 dollars)\n    2  view O-1\n  THE RESPONSES:\n    1  200 OK  <- NOBODY ASKED WHO\n    2  200 OK",
         narration='Here is the real output of the program. [[slnc 400]] First, the requests: a refund, and a view, both with no token. [[slnc 500]] Then the responses. [[slnc 300]] Two hundred, okay. [[slnc 300]] And two hundred, okay again. [[slnc 300]] Nobody asked who.'),
    dict(key='09-act-2-figure', kind='figure', title='Authentication: a password in, a token out',
         body='login_tokens',
         narration="Second demo: authentication. [[slnc 400]] Authentication finds out who is asking. [[slnc 600]] At the top, Ben logs in twice. [[slnc 300]] First with the password guess. [[slnc 300]] Rejected. [[slnc 400]] Then with his real password, ben pass. [[slnc 300]] The shop gives him a session token, tok one. [[slnc 300]] The token remembers three things: ben, his role, customer, and when it was issued, tick zero. [[slnc 600]] A token stands in for the password, for a while. [[slnc 300]] Here tokens are numbered, so they are easy to read. [[slnc 300]] A real token is long and random, so nobody can guess one. [[slnc 600]] At the bottom, three requests to view order O two. [[slnc 300]] No token: four oh one. [[slnc 300]] A made up token, tok ninety nine: four oh one. The shop never issued it. [[slnc 300]] Ben's token, tok one: two hundred, okay. [[slnc 500]] Four oh one means, I do not know who you are."),
    dict(key='10-act-2', kind='console', title='Act Two — Authentication: finding out who is asking',
         body="TWO. Authentication: finding out who is asking.\n  THE REQUESTS:\n    1  log in: ben, password guess\n    2  log in: ben, password ben-pass\n    3  view O-2 with no token\n    4  view O-2 with a made-up token tok-99\n    5  view O-2 with ben's token tok-1\n  THE RESPONSES:\n    1  rejected  <- WRONG PASSWORD\n    2  session tok-1: ben, CUSTOMER, issued at tick 0\n    3  401 UNAUTHENTICATED  <- WHO ARE YOU?\n    4  401 UNAUTHENTICATED  <- NEVER ISSUED\n    5  200 OK  <- IT IS BEN",
         narration="The program's output. [[slnc 400]] First, the five requests: two logins, and three views, with no token, a made up token, and Ben's token. [[slnc 500]] Then the five responses, in the same order. [[slnc 300]] Rejected. [[slnc 300]] A session, tok one. [[slnc 300]] Four oh one. [[slnc 300]] Four oh one. [[slnc 300]] And two hundred, okay."),
    dict(key='11-act-3-figure', kind='figure', title='How the password is checked',
         body='password_hash',
         narration='So how does the shop know that ben pass is right, and guess is wrong? [[slnc 400]] It never stores the password. [[slnc 600]] In green, the section before hashing. [[slnc 300]] When Ben signed up, the shop took his password, and a salt. [[slnc 300]] A salt is a few extra bytes, different for every account. [[slnc 300]] Here it is demo salt ben. [[slnc 500]] P B K D F two mixes the password and the salt, with H M A C S H A two five six, ten thousand times over. [[slnc 300]] That makes each try slow for an attacker. [[slnc 500]] In red, the section after hashing. [[slnc 300]] Thirty two bytes, starting zero f, four five, five f. [[slnc 300]] This hash is all the shop keeps. [[slnc 600]] At login, the typed password goes through the same steps, with the same salt. [[slnc 300]] Guess gives six b, e four. [[slnc 300]] Different, so rejected. [[slnc 300]] Ben pass gives zero f, four five again. [[slnc 300]] The same, so accepted.'),
    dict(key='12-act-3', kind='console', title='Act Three — How a password is checked, step by step',
         body='THREE. How a password is checked, step by step.\n  BEFORE HASHING (when ben signed up):\n    password     ben-pass\n    salt         demo-salt:ben\n    rounds       10000 of PBKDF2 with HMAC-SHA256\n  AFTER HASHING (all the shop keeps, 32 bytes):\n    bytes 1-16   0f455f22 5cf85332 acbcc068 d2a5d78f\n    bytes 17-32  d56a8816 5588d012 179fad0b 15430300\n  AT LOGIN, THE TYPED PASSWORD IS HASHED THE SAME WAY:\n    guess        6be4814d de2bcf7c ...  <- DIFFERENT: REJECTED\n    ben-pass     0f455f22 5cf85332 ...  <- THE SAME: ACCEPTED\n  the password itself is stored nowhere',
         narration='The same steps, printed by the program. [[slnc 400]] Before hashing: the password, the salt, and ten thousand rounds. [[slnc 500]] After hashing: the thirty two bytes the shop keeps. [[slnc 500]] At login: guess gives a different hash, and is rejected. [[slnc 300]] Ben pass gives the same hash, and is accepted. [[slnc 300]] The password itself is stored nowhere.'),
    dict(key='13-act-4-figure', kind='figure', title='A login, and nothing else',
         body='login_only',
         narration="Third demo: a login is not enough. [[slnc 400]] The team thinks the problem is solved, because every request now needs a login. [[slnc 600]] Ben is logged in, with tok one. [[slnc 300]] Ben is a customer. [[slnc 300]] He sends two requests about order O one, which belongs to Ana. [[slnc 300]] Refund it. [[slnc 300]] And view it. [[slnc 600]] The gate asks only one question. [[slnc 300]] Is this person logged in? [[slnc 300]] Yes. [[slnc 500]] So both responses are two hundred, okay. [[slnc 300]] A customer has refunded someone else's laptop, and read someone else's order. [[slnc 600]] Being logged in proved who Ben is. [[slnc 300]] Nothing decided what Ben may do."),
    dict(key='14-act-4', kind='console', title='Act Four — Authenticated is not the same as authorized',
         body="FOUR. Authenticated is not the same as authorized.\n  THE REQUESTS (ben, a customer, token tok-1):\n    1  refund O-1  (ana's order)\n    2  view O-1    (ana's order)\n  THE RESPONSES (the gate asks only: logged in?):\n    1  200 OK  <- LOGGED IN, SO ALLOWED\n    2  200 OK  <- NOT HIS ORDER",
         narration="The program's output. [[slnc 400]] The requests: Ben, with tok one, refunds Ana's order, and views it. [[slnc 500]] The responses: two hundred, okay, twice. [[slnc 300]] Logged in, so allowed. [[slnc 300]] That is the bug."),
    dict(key='15-act-5-figure', kind='figure', title='Two questions: who, then may they',
         body='policy_requests',
         narration="Fourth demo: authorization. [[slnc 400]] Authorization decides what a known person may do. [[slnc 600]] The gate now asks two questions, in order. [[slnc 300]] First, who is this? [[slnc 300]] Then, may this person do this? [[slnc 600]] Eight requests, on the left. [[slnc 300]] Their responses, on the right. [[slnc 500]] The first has no token, so it stops at the first question: four oh one. [[slnc 500]] Ben, a customer, tries to refund. Four oh three, forbidden. [[slnc 300]] He views Ana's order. Forbidden. [[slnc 300]] He views his own order. Okay. [[slnc 500]] Sam, from support, views and refunds. Both okay. [[slnc 300]] Ana cancels Ben's order. Forbidden. [[slnc 300]] Alex, an admin, cancels it. Okay. [[slnc 600]] Four oh three means, I know who you are, and you may not do this."),
    dict(key='16-act-5-1', kind='console', title='Act Five — Authorization: what may this person do?',
         body="FIVE. Authorization: what may this person do?\n  THE REQUESTS:\n    1  no token        refund O-1\n    2  ben, customer   refund O-1\n    3  ben, customer   view O-1  (ana's)\n    4  ben, customer   view O-2  (his own)\n    5  sam, support    view O-1\n    6  sam, support    refund O-1\n    7  ana, customer   cancel O-2  (ben's)\n    8  alex, admin     cancel O-2",
         narration="The program's output, in two parts. [[slnc 400]] First, the eight requests: who sends each one, and what it asks for."),
    dict(key='17-act-5-2', kind='console', title='Act Five — Authorization: what may this person do?',
         body='FIVE. (continued)\n  THE RESPONSES:\n    1  401 UNAUTHENTICATED  <- NOBODY KNOWN\n    2  403 FORBIDDEN  <- KNOWN, NOT ALLOWED\n    3  403 FORBIDDEN\n    4  200 OK\n    5  200 OK\n    6  200 OK\n    7  403 FORBIDDEN\n    8  200 OK',
         narration='Then the eight responses, in the same order. [[slnc 300]] One four oh one, for nobody. [[slnc 300]] Three four oh threes, for known people who may not. [[slnc 300]] And four okays.'),
    dict(key='18-act-6-figure', kind='figure', title='How the gate decides one request',
         body='gate_steps',
         narration="So how exactly does the gate decide? [[slnc 400]] Here is one request, step by step. [[slnc 600]] At the top, the rules: one small table. [[slnc 300]] A customer may view and cancel, only their own orders. [[slnc 300]] Support may view and refund any order. [[slnc 300]] An admin may do anything. [[slnc 600]] The request: token tok one asks to view order O one. [[slnc 500]] Step one, authentication. [[slnc 300]] The gate looks up tok one. [[slnc 300]] It is Ben's session, role customer, issued at tick zero. [[slnc 300]] Its age is zero ticks, under the lifetime of three. [[slnc 300]] So it is live, and the person is Ben. [[slnc 600]] Step two, authorization. [[slnc 300]] The rule for a customer who views is, own only. [[slnc 300]] The owner of O one is Ana, not Ben. [[slnc 500]] So the response is four oh three, forbidden."),
    dict(key='19-act-6-1', kind='console', title='Act Six — How the gate decides, step by step',
         body='SIX. How the gate decides, step by step.\n  THE RULES (from PolicyGate.allowed):\n    role        VIEW        CANCEL      REFUND\n    CUSTOMER    own only    own only    no\n    SUPPORT     any         no          any\n    ADMIN       any         any         any\n  THE REQUEST:\n    token tok-1, view O-1',
         narration='The program prints the same steps. [[slnc 400]] First, the rules, read from the code itself. [[slnc 300]] Then the request.'),
    dict(key='20-act-6-2', kind='console', title='Act Six — How the gate decides, step by step',
         body='SIX. (continued)\n  STEP 1. AUTHENTICATION: WHO IS THIS?\n    session tok-1  ben, CUSTOMER, issued at tick 0\n    age 0 ticks, under 3: live, so it is ben\n  STEP 2. AUTHORIZATION: MAY BEN DO THIS?\n    rule for CUSTOMER, VIEW: own only\n    owner of O-1: ana, not ben\n  THE RESPONSE:\n    403 FORBIDDEN  <- KNOWN, NOT ALLOWED',
         narration='Step one: the session, its age, and who it is. [[slnc 400]] Step two: the rule, and the owner. [[slnc 400]] And the response: four oh three.'),
    dict(key='21-act-7-figure', kind='figure', title='What the checks cost',
         body='the_bill',
         narration="Last, the bill. [[slnc 400]] The checks cost something. [[slnc 600]] At the top, a timeline. [[slnc 300]] Sam logs in at tick zero. [[slnc 300]] His session lives for three ticks. [[slnc 300]] At tick four, its age is four, which is not under three. [[slnc 300]] Expired. [[slnc 300]] His refund gets four oh one. [[slnc 300]] Safer if a token is stolen, and a new login for Sam. [[slnc 600]] At the bottom, a subtler cost. [[slnc 300]] Before the demotion, Sam's session holds the role support. [[slnc 300]] After it, the directory says customer, but the session still says support. [[slnc 500]] A gate that uses the role copied at login lets Sam refund. [[slnc 300]] Two hundred, okay. A stale role. [[slnc 300]] A gate that looks the role up says four oh three. [[slnc 300]] But that costs one lookup, on every request. [[slnc 500]] Fast, or up to date. [[slnc 300]] Either way, it is a choice."),
    dict(key='22-act-7', kind='console', title='Act Seven — The bill: sessions expire, and roles go stale',
         body='SEVEN. The bill: sessions expire, and roles go stale.\n  SESSIONS EXPIRE:\n    sam logs in at tick 0: tok-1, lifetime 3 ticks\n    at tick 4 the age is 4, not under 3: expired\n    sam refunds O-1: 401 UNAUTHENTICATED\n  BEFORE THE DEMOTION:\n    sam logs in: session tok-1 holds role SUPPORT\n  AFTER THE DEMOTION (alex makes sam a CUSTOMER):\n    refund, role copied at login: 200 OK  <- STALE\n    refund, role looked up now:   403 FORBIDDEN\n    directory lookups: 0 for the first, 1 for the second',
         narration="The program's output. [[slnc 400]] First, the session that expires at tick four, and the four oh one. [[slnc 500]] Then before the demotion: Sam's session holds support. [[slnc 400]] After it: the copied role gives two hundred, the looked up role gives four oh three. [[slnc 300]] Zero lookups, against one."),
    dict(key='23-order', kind='bullets', title='The Gate Asks In Order',
         body=['1. Is there a live session?', '     no: 401', '2. Does the rule allow this person?', '     no: 403', '3. Only then: act', '', 'On every endpoint, from one policy.'],
         narration="Let's put the demos in order. [[slnc 400]] The gate asks two questions, in this order. [[slnc 500]] One. Is there a live session? [[slnc 300]] If not, four oh one. [[slnc 500]] Two. Does the rule allow this person to do this? [[slnc 300]] If not, four oh three. [[slnc 500]] Only then does the shop act. [[slnc 600]] And this happens on every endpoint, from one policy, so the rules live in one place."),
    dict(key='24-recognise', kind='bullets', title='Where You Have Seen It',
         body=['A 401 sign-in page, a 403 no-access page', 'Your session has expired', 'Viewer, editor, admin', 'Remember me'],
         narration='You have probably met these already. [[slnc 400]] A page that asks you to sign in, and a page that says you do not have permission, are the four oh one and the four oh three. [[slnc 500]] A message saying your session has expired is the cost of a short session. [[slnc 500]] Roles such as viewer, editor, and admin are authorization. [[slnc 500]] And a remember me box trades a longer session for convenience.'),
    dict(key='25-verdict', kind='bullets', title='How To Use It',
         body=['Check both, in order, in one place', '401 for nobody, 403 for not allowed', 'The gate goes on every endpoint', 'Choose: roles copied, or looked up'],
         narration='So, how do you use this? [[slnc 400]] Check both, in order, in one place. [[slnc 500]] Four oh one when nobody is known. [[slnc 300]] Four oh three when a known person may not. [[slnc 500]] Put the gate in front of every endpoint, because one open endpoint undoes the rest. [[slnc 500]] And choose, on purpose, whether a role is copied into the session or looked up each time.'),
    dict(key='26-classes', kind='bullets', title='The Java Classes',
         body=['AuthDemo: runs the seven acts', 'Credentials: keeps only salted password hashes', 'Authenticator: authentication: logins and session tokens', 'LoginOnlyGate: the mistake: a login and nothing else', 'PolicyGate: authentication, then authorization', 'UserDirectory: the current role of every account', 'Status: 200, 401 and 403'],
         narration='Now, a quick look at the code. [[slnc 300]] Eight small classes. [[slnc 300]] Here is what the main ones do.'),
    dict(key='27-class-1', kind='code', title='AuthDemo — the seven acts',
         body='// AuthDemo: runs the seven acts\nact1NoChecks(out);\nact2Authentication(out);\nact3PasswordCheck(out);\nact4LoginIsNotEnough(out);\nact5Authorization(out);\nact6HowTheGateDecides(out);\nact7Bill(out);',
         narration='Auth demo runs the seven acts, one method each. [[slnc 300]] Every act builds a fresh shop, with the same four people and two orders, so the acts do not affect each other.'),
    dict(key='28-class-2', kind='code', title='Credentials — hash, and verify',
         body='// Credentials: keeps only salted password hashes\nbyte[] salt = salt(username).getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8);\nPBEKeySpec spec = new PBEKeySpec(password.toCharArray(),\n        salt, ITERATIONS, 256);\nreturn SecretKeyFactory.getInstance("PBKDF2WithHmacSHA256")\n        .generateSecret(spec).getEncoded();\n...\nbyte[] stored = hashes.get(username);\n...\nbyte[] attempt = hash(username, password);   // same salt and rounds\nreturn stored != null && MessageDigest.isEqual(stored, attempt);',
         narration='Credentials never keeps a password. [[slnc 300]] Hash mixes the password and the salt with P B K D F two, ten thousand rounds, into thirty two bytes. [[slnc 300]] Verify hashes the typed password the same way, and compares the two hashes. [[slnc 300]] That is act three.'),
    dict(key='29-class-3', kind='code', title='Authenticator — login, then authenticate each request',
         body='// Authenticator: authentication: logins and session tokens\nif (!credentials.verify(username, password)) {\n    return Optional.empty();                 // wrong password\n}\n...\nSession session = new Session("tok-" + (++issued), username,\n        account.role(), ticker.now());\n...\nif (session == null\n        || ticker.now() - session.issuedAt() >= lifetimeTicks) {\n    return Optional.empty();                 // unknown or expired',
         narration='The authenticator answers the first question, who is this. [[slnc 300]] Login checks the password, and issues a session with the user, the role, and the time. [[slnc 300]] Authenticate turns a token back into a session, but only if it exists and is younger than its lifetime.'),
    dict(key='30-class-4', kind='code', title='LoginOnlyGate — any live session passes',
         body='// LoginOnlyGate: the mistake: a login and nothing else\nreturn authenticator.authenticate(token)\n        .map(s -> new Response(Status.OK,\n                "logged in as " + s.username()))\n        .orElseGet(() -> new Response(Status.UNAUTHENTICATED,\n                "no live session"));',
         narration='The login only gate asks just the first question. [[slnc 300]] Any live session gets okay, whatever it asks for. [[slnc 300]] That is the bug from act four.'),
    dict(key='31-class-5', kind='code', title='PolicyGate — check: 401, then 403',
         body='// PolicyGate: authentication, then authorization\nOptional<Session> session = authenticator.authenticate(token);\nif (session.isEmpty()) {\n    return new Response(Status.UNAUTHENTICATED,\n            "no live session");\n}\n...\nRole role = roleSource == RoleSource.SESSION\n        ? session.get().roleAtLogin()        // copied at login\n        : directory.find(user).role();       // looked up now\nif (allowed(role, user, action, order)) {\n...\nreturn new Response(Status.FORBIDDEN,     // known, not allowed',
         narration='The policy gate asks both questions, in order. [[slnc 300]] No live session, four oh one. [[slnc 300]] Then it takes the role, copied at login or looked up now, and asks the rules. [[slnc 300]] If they say no, four oh three.'),
    dict(key='32-class-6', kind='code', title='PolicyGate — allowed: the rules, in one place',
         body='// PolicyGate: authentication, then authorization\nboolean owner = order.owner().equals(user);\nreturn switch (role) {\n    case ADMIN -> true;\n    case SUPPORT -> action == Action.VIEW || action == Action.REFUND;\n    case CUSTOMER -> owner\n            && (action == Action.VIEW || action == Action.CANCEL);\n};',
         narration='Allowed holds all the rules, in one switch. [[slnc 300]] An admin may do anything. [[slnc 300]] Support may view and refund. [[slnc 300]] A customer may view and cancel, only when they own the order. [[slnc 300]] The table in act six is printed from this method.'),
    dict(key='33-class-7', kind='code', title='UserDirectory — find counts the lookups',
         body='// UserDirectory: the current role of every account\npublic Account find(String username) {\n    lookups++;\n    return accounts.get(username);\n}',
         narration='The user directory holds the current role of every account. [[slnc 300]] Each find is counted, which is how act seven shows the price of looking the role up.'),
    dict(key='34-class-8', kind='code', title='Status — two different failures',
         body='// Status: 200, 401 and 403\nOK(200),\nUNAUTHENTICATED(401),\nFORBIDDEN(403);',
         narration='And status names the answers. [[slnc 300]] Four oh one for, I do not know who you are. [[slnc 300]] Four oh three for, I know you, and no.'),
    dict(key='35-outro', kind='outro', title='Thanks for Watching',
         body=['Full source, notes, diagrams and an animated walkthrough', 'are in the repository. Add a logout step', 'and see the token get a 401.'],
         narration="That's authentication and authorization. [[slnc 400]] If you remember one sentence, make it this one. [[slnc 300]] Authentication finds out who you are, authorization decides what you may do, and a gate must do both. [[slnc 500]] The full source code, written notes, diagrams, and an animated walkthrough are all in the repository. [[slnc 300]] It runs on your own machine, with nothing installed except a Java development kit. [[slnc 500]] Here is one exercise to try. [[slnc 300]] Add a logout step to the last demo, and watch the token get a four oh one. [[slnc 500]] If you found this useful, a like really helps other people discover it. [[slnc 300]] Kindly subscribe to the channel. [[slnc 300]] It helps the channel grow and bring you more content. [[slnc 400]] Thanks for watching!"),
]

VIDEO = {'footer': 'Authentication vs Authorization  ·  Security Basics  ·  Java', 'poster': {'headline': [('AUTHENTICATION', 'text'), ('VS AUTHORIZATION', 'accent')], 'taglines': [('Who are you, and what may you do?', 'gold')], 'before': {'code': 'if (loggedIn) allow()', 'note': 'any customer can refund', 'tag': 'BEFORE'}, 'after': {'code': 'gate.check(token, action)', 'note': '401, then 403, then 200', 'tag': 'AFTER'}}, 'highlight': {'console_green': ['THE REQUEST', 'BEFORE HASHING', 'BEFORE THE DEMOTION'], 'console_rose': ['THE RESPONSE', 'AFTER HASHING', 'AFTER THE DEMOTION'], 'console_amber': ['STEP ', 'AT LOGIN', 'THE RULES', 'SESSIONS EXPIRE']}}
